-->Same name as class
-->No return type
-->Used to assign values

final-->Once assigned, you cannot change it.
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final int x = 10;
        // x = 20; ❌ ERROR (cannot change)
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}

finally-->This block will run no matter what (exception or not)
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int a = 10 / 0;  // error
            } 
        catch (Exception e) {
             System.out.println("Exception caught");
            } 
        finally {
            System.out.println("Finally block always runs");
        }
    }
}

finalize()-->Method → called before object is destroyed.
class Demo {
    protected void finalize() {
        System.out.println("Object is destroyed");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo d1 = new Demo();
        d1 = null;   // making object eligible for garbage collection
        System.gc(); // request JVM to clean memory
    }
}

Comparison :
| Feature   | final                   | finally             | finalize()          |
| --------- | ----------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Type      | Keyword                 | Block               | Method              |
| Purpose   | Restrict changes        | Always execute code | Cleanup before GC   |
| Used with | Variable, method, class | try-catch           | Object (GC process) |

final stops changes, 
finally always runs and
finalize() is for cleanup before an object is removed.

Garbage Collection (GC) :
It is a process where Java automatically removes unused objects from memory.

example code : 
class Demo {
    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Object is alive");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Demo d1 = new Demo();  // object created
        d1.show();
        d1 = null;  // object is now unused (eligible for GC)
        System.gc();  // request JVM to run garbage collection
    }
}

-->Java handles memory automatically (no need to free manually)
-->System.gc() is only a request, not a guarantee
-->You don’t control exactly when GC runs





